<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ho dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1407 CE · capital Tay Do</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A seven-year Vietnamese dynasty of radical reformer Ho Quy Ly, who issued paper money and built a stone citadel before the Ming conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-quy-ly">Ho Quy Ly</a>, a powerful minister related by marriage to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tran-dynasty">Tran dynasty</a>, dominated the court in the 1390s, moved the capital to a new stone citadel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadel-of-the-ho-dynasty">Tay Do</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-hoa">Thanh Hoa</a> and in 1400 deposed the last Tran emperor. He renamed the state Dai Ngu and soon passed the throne to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-han-thuong">Ho Han Thuong</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-quy-ly">Ho Quy Ly</a> pursued sweeping reforms: he limited private landholdings and the number of slaves, introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>, reformed the examinations to favour practical learning, and promoted chu nom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">The Citadel</a> of the Ho Dynasty, built of huge cut stone blocks, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. His usurpation alienated many Tran loyalists.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Using the overthrow of the Tran as a pretext, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> sent a Ming army in 1406. Dai Ngu collapsed within a year; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-quy-ly">Ho Quy Ly</a> and his son were captured in 1407 and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi-province">Jiaozhi Province</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ho-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ho dynasty" width="320" height="451"><figcaption>Image: Map-of-southeast-asia 1400 CE.png: Javierfv1212 Map-of-southeast-asia 900 CE-es.svg: Rowanwindwhistler derivative wor... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map-of-southeast-asia_1400_CE-es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1407 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tay Do</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ho Quy Ly, Ho Han Thuong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese, Classical Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dai Ngu, Đại Ngu, Nhà Hồ</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1404&amp;focus=ho-dynasty" aria-label="Ho dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7541 1416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ho dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7861 1672L7846 1656M7861 1672L7643 1242"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7846 1656h0M7643 1242h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7861 1672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7861 1672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1404&amp;focus=ho-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
